  "name": "Example: Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis",
  "problem": "Express the Cartesian point $P (0,-7)$ in polar coordinates.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tr &= \\sqrt{0^2 + (-7)^2}\\\n\t\t&= \\sqrt{7^2}\\\n\t\t&= 7\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of the $y$-axis, we have $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(7, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
